at; // Surefire reruns this class from the start, so it opens and closes Playwright itself.publicclassOfferTest { privatestaticint attempt = 0; // the rerun happens in the same JVMstaticPlaywright playwright; staticBrowser browser; BrowserContext context; Page page; @BeforeAllstaticvoid launch() { playwright = Playwright.create(); browser = playwright.chromium().launch(); } @BeforeEachvoid newPage() { context = browser.newContext(newBrowser.NewContextOptions().setBaseURL(\"http://localhost:4173\")); page = context.newPage(); } @Testvoid theOfferShowsUp() { attempt++; System.out.println(\"attempt \" + attempt); page.navigate(\"/widgets\"); page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, newPage.GetByRoleOptions().setName(\"Load offers\")).click(); // Flaky on purpose: the first try waits only 0.5 s, but the offer needs 1.2 s.double wait = attempt == 1 ? 500 : 5_000; assertThat(page.getByTestId(\"offer\")).isVisible(newLocatorAssertions.IsVisibleOptions().setTimeout(wait)); } @AfterEachvoid closeContext() { context.close(); } @AfterAllstaticvoid close() { playwright.close(); } } Terminal $ mvn test -Dsurefire.rerunFailingTestsCount=2 Terminal output [INFO] ------------------------------------------------------- [INFO] T E S T S [INFO] ------------------------------------------------------- [INFO] Running com.brainy.OfferTest attempt 1 [ERROR] Tests run: 1, Failures: 1, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0, Time elapsed: 4.556 s <<< FAILURE! -- in com.brainy.OfferTest [ERROR] com.brainy.OfferTest.theOfferShowsUp -- Time elapsed: 2.005 s <<< FAILURE! org.opentest4j.AssertionFailedError: Locator expected to be visible Error: element(s) not found Call log: - Assert \"isVisible\" getByTestId(\"offer\") with timeout 500ms - waiting for getByTestId(\"offer\") [INFO] Running com.brainy.OfferTest attempt 2 [INFO] Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0, Time elapsed: 3.527 s -- in com.brainy.OfferTest [WARNING] Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0, Flakes: 1 [INFO] [INFO] ------------------------------------------------------------------------ [INFO] BUILD SUCCESS 📄 tests/test_offer.py from playwright.sync_api importPage, expect def test_the_offer_shows_up(page: Page, request): attempt = request.node.execution_count # set by pytest-rerunfailures print(f\"\\nattempt {attempt}\") page.goto(\"/widgets\") page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Load offers\").click() # Flaky on purpose: the first try waits only 0.5 s, but the offer needs 1.2 s. wait = 500if attempt == 1else5_000 expect(page.get_by_test_id(\"offer\")).to_be_visible(timeout=wait) Terminal $ pytest -v -s --reruns 2 Terminal output collecting ... collected 1 item tests/test_offer.py::test_the_offer_shows_up[chromium] attempt 1 RERUN tests/test_offer.py::test_the_offer_shows_up[chromium] attempt 2 PASSED ========================== 1 passed, 1 rerun in 6.18s ========================== 📄 tests/offer.spec.js import { test, expect } from'@playwright/test'; test('the offer shows up', async ({ page }, testInfo) => { console.log(`attempt ${testInfo.retry + 1}`); await page.goto('/widgets'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Load offers' }).click(); // Flaky on purpose: the first try waits only 0.5 s, but the offer needs 1.2 s.const wait = testInfo.retry === 0 ? 500 : 5_000; await expect(page.getByTestId('offer')).toBeVisible({ timeout: wait }); }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 1 test using 1 worker attempt"],[9,"retries","Retries and flaky tests","1 ✘ 1 [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.js:3:1 › the offer shows up (2.0s) attempt 2 ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.js:3:1 › the offer shows up (retry #1) (2.8s) 1) [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.js:3:1 › the offer shows up ───────────────────────────────────── Error: expect(locator).toBeVisible() failed Locator: getByTestId('offer') Expected: visible Timeout: 500ms Error: element(s) not found Call log: - Expect \"toBeVisible\" getByTestId('offer') with timeout 500ms - waiting for getByTestId('offer') 7 | // Flaky on purpose: the first try waits only 0.5 s, but the offer needs 1.2 s. 8 | const wait = testInfo.retry === 0 ? 500 : 5_000; > 9 | await expect(page.getByTestId('offer')).toBeVisible({ timeout: wait }); | ^ 10 | }); 11 | at tests/offer.spec.js:9:43 Error Context: test-results/offer-the-offer-shows-up-chromium/error-context.md 1 flaky [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.js:3:1 › the offer shows up ────────────────────────────────────── 📄 tests/offer.spec.ts import { test, expect } from'@playwright/test'; test('the offer shows up', async ({ page }, testInfo) => { console.log(`attempt ${testInfo.retry + 1}`); await page.goto('/widgets'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Load offers' }).click(); // Flaky on purpose: the first try waits only 0.5 s, but the offer needs 1.2 s.const wait = testInfo.retry === 0 ? 500 : 5_000; await expect(page.getByTestId('offer')).toBeVisible({ timeout: wait }); }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 1 test using 1 worker attempt 1 ✘ 1 [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.ts:3:1 › the offer shows up (1.9s) attempt 2 ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.ts:3:1 › the offer shows up (retry #1) (2.6s) 1) [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.ts:3:1 › the offer shows up ───────────────────────────────────── Error: expect(locator).toBeVisible() failed Locator: getByTestId('offer') Expected: visible Timeout: 500ms Error: element(s) not found Call log: - Expect \"toBeVisible\" getByTestId('offer') with timeout 500ms - waiting for getByTestId('offer') 7 | // Flaky on purpose: the first try waits only 0.5 s, but the offer needs 1.2 s. 8 | const wait = testInfo.retry === 0 ? 500 : 5_000; > 9 | await expect(page.getByTestId('offer')).toBeVisible({ timeout: wait }); | ^ 10 | }); 11 | at tests/offer.spec.ts:9:43 Error Context: test-results/offer-the-offer-shows-up-chromium/error-context.md 1 flaky [chromium] › tests/offer.spec.ts:3:1 › the offer shows up ────────────────────────────────────── What happens The first try failed, the retry passed, and the run still passed. Each runner marks the test so you notice: Playwright Test says 1 flaky, pytest says 1 rerun and Surefire says Flakes: 1. A flaky test is a bug in the test or the app, and the retry only hides it until someone fixes it. In our run, a Surefire rerun of a class that uses @UsePlaywright failed with Playwright connection closed, so the Java version opens and closes Playwright in its own @BeforeAll and @AfterAll. Playwright Test pytest JUnit 5 with Maven set retries retries: 2, or --retries=2 the pytest-rerunfailures plugin, --reruns 2 mvn test -Dsurefire.rerunFailingTestsCount=2 which try is this testInfo.retry, starts at 0 request.node.execution_count, starts at 1 not given, count it yourself how it is reported flaky rerun Flakes fail the run on flaky tests --fail-on-flaky-tests not built in not built in 🏢 Most teams turn retries on only in CI, for example retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0, and keep a list of flaky tests to fix. A retry also records a trace in Playwright Test when trace is on-first-retry, which is often the fastest way to find the cause. Lesson 4 of 5"],[9,"timeouts","Timeouts","Every wait in Playwright has a limit, so a broken test fails instead of hanging forever. There are several limits, and each one has its own default. Limit Default Playwright Test Python Java one test 30 s timeout: 30_000, test.setTimeout(), test.slow() not built in (the pytest-timeout plugin adds it) JUnit @Timeout(30) one expect check 5 s expect: { timeout: 5_000 } expect.set_options(timeout=5_000) PlaywrightAssertions.setDefaultAssertionTimeout(5_000) one action no limit in Playwright Test, 30 s in the libraries use: { actionTimeout } page.set_default_timeout() page.setDefaultTimeout() one navigation no limit in Playwright Test, 30 s in the libraries use: { navigationTimeout } page.set_default_navigation_timeout() page.setDefaultNavigationTimeout() the whole run no limit globalTimeout not built in not built in Timeouts that are long enough, and one that is too short · 💥 ts